In Unity3D, there are several ways to make a GameObject not receive touch events. Here are the most common approaches:

## 1. **Remove or Disable Colliders**
Touch events in Unity require colliders to detect touches. Simply remove or disable the collider:

```csharp
// Disable the collider
GetComponent<Collider>().enabled = false;
// or for 2D
GetComponent<Collider2D>().enabled = false;
```

## 2. **Use Layer-Based Raycasting**
Set the GameObject to a layer that your touch/raycast system ignores:

```csharp
// Set GameObject to a specific layer (e.g., "IgnoreRaycast")
gameObject.layer = LayerMask.NameToLayer("IgnoreRaycast");
```

Then ensure your touch detection code excludes this layer:

```csharp
// Example touch detection that ignores certain layers
int layerMask = ~(1 << LayerMask.NameToLayer("IgnoreRaycast"));
RaycastHit hit;
if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out hit, Mathf.Infinity, layerMask))
{
    // Process touch
}
```

## 3. **Use CanvasGroup (for UI elements)**
For UI elements, use CanvasGroup component:

```csharp
// Add CanvasGroup component and disable interaction
CanvasGroup canvasGroup = gameObject.AddComponent<CanvasGroup>();
canvasGroup.interactable = false;
canvasGroup.blocksRaycasts = false;
```

## 4. **Disable EventSystem Interaction (for UI)**
For UI elements, you can also disable the GraphicRaycaster:

```csharp
// Disable raycasting on UI elements
GetComponent<GraphicRaycaster>().enabled = false;
```

## 5. **Custom Touch Handler with Ignore List**
Create a custom touch manager that maintains an ignore list:

```csharp
public class TouchManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    private HashSet<GameObject> ignoredObjects = new HashSet<GameObject>();

    public void IgnoreGameObject(GameObject obj)
    {
        ignoredObjects.Add(obj);
    }

    public void UnignoreGameObject(GameObject obj)
    {
        ignoredObjects.Remove(obj);
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (Input.touchCount > 0 || Input.GetMouseButtonDown(0))
        {
            Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(Input.mousePosition);
            RaycastHit hit;

            if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out hit))
            {
                if (!ignoredObjects.Contains(hit.collider.gameObject))
                {
                    // Process touch event
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## 6. **Using Tags**
Set a specific tag and check for it in your touch handling code:

```csharp
// Set tag
gameObject.tag = "IgnoreTouch";

// In your touch handler
if (hit.collider.gameObject.tag != "IgnoreTouch")
{
    // Process touch
}
```

## Complete Example Script
Here's a script you can attach to any GameObject to control its touch interaction:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class TouchInteraction : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private bool receivesTouchEvents = true;
    private Collider objectCollider;
    private Collider2D objectCollider2D;

    void Start()
    {
        objectCollider = GetComponent<Collider>();
        objectCollider2D = GetComponent<Collider2D>();

        SetTouchInteraction(receivesTouchEvents);
    }

    public void SetTouchInteraction(bool enabled)
    {
        receivesTouchEvents = enabled;

        // Handle 3D collider
        if (objectCollider != null)
            objectCollider.enabled = enabled;

        // Handle 2D collider
        if (objectCollider2D != null)
            objectCollider2D.enabled = enabled;

        // For UI elements
        var canvasGroup = GetComponent<CanvasGroup>();
        if (canvasGroup != null)
        {
            canvasGroup.interactable = enabled;
            canvasGroup.blocksRaycasts = enabled;
        }
    }
}
```

Choose the method that best fits your project's architecture and requirements. The most straightforward approach is usually to disable the collider or use layers for more complex scenarios.

